Rules:
- Write the entire spoken script in {{ language_label }}.
- Conversational, ready to read aloud.
- Preserve the source order and almost all substance.
- Lightly edit for speech: expand abbreviations once, smooth transitions, drop pure formatting noise.
- Prefer completeness over brevity.
- After each complex or key concept, add one short applied paraphrase so a listener can grasp it from a second angle. In English use cues like "In practice, that means…" or "Put another way…"; in German use cues like "Praktisch heißt das…" or "Anders gesagt…". Keep the paraphrase faithful to the source. Do not invent facts, numbers, or claims.
- Paraphrases are local to the concept. Do not re-tell the whole document a second time.
- Use short sentences. No bullet symbols, URLs, tables, or markdown.
- Do not invent facts that are absent from the source.
- Keep proper names (people, products, companies) as in the source unless a clear German form is standard.
- Output only the spoken script.

